The Huawei protocol implementation in
modules/huawei-wear/android/src/main/java/nodomain/freeyourgadget/gadgetbridge/
and the Kotlin session layer in modules/huawei-wear/android/src/main/java/com/joyodigitama/huaweiwear/,
and their Swift port in modules/huawei-wear/ios/HuaweiProtocol/ (the iOS protocol layer),
are derived from Gadgetbridge (https://codeberg.org/Freeyourgadget/Gadgetbridge),
upstream commit bb7a1e83a29837153bf0c3a50e9869dec3dac0dd, licensed AGPL-3.0-or-later.
Original copyright notices are retained in each ported file; modified files carry a
"Modified for watch-fit" line.

RouteFile.kt and RouteFile.swift (the route file sent to the watch) are ports of Gadgetbridge's HuaweiConvertTrack.java and
HuaweiRouteTrack.java, and the route push in P2pController follows its HuaweiP2PFitnessData.java; verbatim copies of the two
converter classes live in modules/huawei-wear/ios/HuaweiProtocol/GoldenGen/vendor/ only to generate the test fixtures.

The importer for Huawei's Privacy Centre health export (HuaweiExport*.kt, HuaweiHealthDetailParser.kt,
HuaweiMotionPathParser.kt in modules/huawei-wear/android/) was written for this project. Its idea of the export
layout (folders, record keys, the motion-path track records, the pause marker and the invalid-JSON quirks) comes from
reading what these MIT-licensed community tools document; no code was copied from them:
  m1ty/huawei2gpx (https://github.com/m1ty/huawei2gpx),
  JordyThien/Huawei-Health-Importer (https://github.com/JordyThien/Huawei-Health-Importer),
  driemekasten/hhealth2zepp (https://github.com/driemekasten/hhealth2zepp).
It uses zip4j (https://github.com/srikanth-lingala/zip4j, Apache-2.0) to read the AES-encrypted ZIP.

